  "step_3": "The decision reason requires (1) treating outcome differences as negligible and (2) preferring the lottery with the higher probability of a favorable outcome. Step 1 shows that Lottery B has a higher probability of a favorable outcome (25% vs 20%), so the reason would choose B. In Step 2, the individual acknowledges the probabilities and notes that B's is only slightly lower—however, this contradicts the actual probabilities (B is higher, not lower). This may be a slip in expression or misunderstanding. If taken literally, the individual believes B has a lower chance, which is incorrect. Still, the focus is on the probability of winning, suggesting attention to the relevant variable. However, there is no indication that the individual considered the outcome difference (4000 vs 3000) and judged it negligible—this part of the reason is not reflected. Moreover, the justification does not state a preference for higher probability; instead, it minimizes the difference, possibly implying indifference or compensatory reasoning. Therefore, while probability of winning is considered, the full logic of the decision reason—especially the rule to prefer higher probability under negligible outcome differences—is not clearly present. The mismatch in factual accuracy (saying B has lower probability when it is higher) and absence of explicit indifference to outcomes reduce confidence.",
  "final_assessment_score": 40
}

